/*=============================================================================
🧠 SYSTEMVERILOG — MAILBOX CONCEPT 
=============================================================================

📘 Purpose:
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
A mailbox provides **safe, synchronized communication** between two or moreparallel processes (like Generator → Driver) in SystemVerilog. (or)
-Mailbox is a communication channel in between two or more parallel processes in systemverilog.

-Instead of using global variables or delays, mailboxes act as a **thread-safeFIFO queue** that handles timing and synchronization automatically.

Used widely in **UVM** for:
  - Sequencer → Driver communication
  - Monitor → Scoreboard packet transfer


-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
🔹 1️⃣ Declaration and Creation
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
mailbox mbx;            // declare a mailbox handle
mbx = new();            // create an *unbounded* mailbox

// or bounded mailbox (fixed size)
mailbox #(packet) mbx = new(5);  // holds 5 items max


-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
🔹 2️⃣ Common Methods
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
put(item)       → puts item into mailbox (waits if full in bounded type)
get(item)       → retrieves next item (waits if empty)
try_put(item)   → non-blocking put; returns 0 if full
try_get(item)   → non-blocking get; returns 0 if empty
num()           → returns current number of items


-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
🔹 3️⃣ Producer–Consumer Example (Generator → Driver)
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
class packet;
  rand bit [7:0] data;
endclass

module tb;
  mailbox mbx = new(); // shared mailbox between threads

  // Generator process
  initial begin
    packet p;
    repeat (5) begin
      p = new();               // create new object
      p.randomize();           // assign random data
      mbx.put(p);              // send to mailbox
      $display("GEN: Sent packet %0d", p.data);
    end
  end

  // Driver process
  initial begin
    packet q;
    repeat (5) begin
      mbx.get(q);              // get from mailbox (blocks if empty)
      $display("DRV: Got packet %0d", q.data);
    end
  end
endmodule


-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
🔹 4️⃣ Simulation Behavior
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
- Both `initial` blocks run **concurrently** (parallel processes).
- When Generator executes `mbx.put(p)`:
    → The Driver, if waiting at `get(q)`, immediately wakes up.
- This synchronization is **automatic** (no #delays needed).
- Each put–get pair occurs in lockstep using **delta cycles**.

💡 Even though no time delays exist, multiple delta cycles occur internally.
All events still happen at simulation time = 0 ns (you can check with $time).


-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
🔹 5️⃣ $timeformat(-9, 0, " ns", 10);
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
Controls display formatting for `$time` in simulation logs:
  - `-9` → Display in nanoseconds (10⁻⁹)
  - `0`  → No decimal precision
  - `" ns"` → Append “ns” as a unit label
  - `10` → Field width for alignment in log output

Example:
  [       0 ns] Start simulation


-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
🔹 6️⃣ Bounded vs Unbounded Mailboxes
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------

• Unbounded Mailbox (`new()`):
    - Can hold unlimited packets.
    - If generator does 5 puts and driver only 3 gets:
        → 2 packets remain queued inside mailbox (not lost).

• Bounded Mailbox (`new(N)`):
    - Can store at most N packets.
    - If generator tries to add more (N+1):
        → `put()` **blocks** until driver performs `get()`.

🧩 Think of mailbox as a “buffered postbox”: Generator drops letters (put), Driver picks them up (get).
If Driver is slow → queue fills up (bounded).
If Driver stops → letters stay inside (unbounded).


-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
🔹 7️⃣ Doubts Explained
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------

❓1: Why must both generator and driver use same repeat count?
✅ Because:
   - Generator produces 5 packets → Driver should consume 5.
   - If mismatch:
       • Generator > Driver → leftover packets remain in mailbox.
       • Driver > Generator → driver blocks forever waiting for more data.

❓2: Does control switch to driver right after put()?
✅ Yes.
   - As soon as `put()` executes, any process waiting on `get()` is woken.
   - They alternate execution automatically via delta cycles.

❓3: How long do these processes run?
✅ No real simulation time elapses (no #delay).
   - All transactions occur at time = 0 ns.
   - Simulator uses **delta cycles** internally to synchronize both threads.

❓4: Why driver does not call new() for packet?
✅ Because `get(q)` receives the **object reference** (handle) from the mailbox.
   - Generator created it using `new()`.
   - Mailbox transfers the handle (not a copy).
   - So driver already has access to the same packet object.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
🔹 8️⃣ Real UVM Use Case
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
- Sequencer → Driver:  uses mailbox/TLM to send randomized transactions.
- Monitor → Scoreboard:  passes observed DUT data for comparison.
- Ensures no timing race, thread-safe communication, and modular design.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
🔹 9️⃣ Key Takeaways
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
✅ Mailbox = synchronized, thread-safe FIFO between parallel processes.
✅ No #delay needed → simulator handles synchronization.
✅ `put()` and `get()` work in lockstep.
✅ Unbounded mailbox can hold extra packets; bounded mailbox waits.
✅ Mailbox transfers *object handles*, not copies.

